{"assessment":"The source packet provides a complete experimental and theoretical description of the unitary almost-Mathieu operator (UAMO) and its non-Hermitian extension (PUAMO) realized via single-photon quantum walks. The selected quantitative cluster focuses on the Lyapunov exponent L_lambda1_lambda2_eta of the PUAMO, given by L = max(0, log(lambda_0) - 2*pi*|eta|) with lambda_0 = lambda_2*(1+lambda_1')/(lambda_1*(1+lambda_2')). Two claims are built for the private regimes eta=0 and eta=0.1, sharing public controls lambda_1 and lambda_2 in [0.2,0.67] and the common readout lyapunov_exponent. The source reports the full parameter-space phase scans for the Hermitian (eta=0) and non-Hermitian (eta=0.1) cases, and the Lyapunov formula is explicitly given in the supplementary material. The extraction captures the central quantitative relation with all structural constants as named, anchored parameters. The source contains internal inconsistencies in the PT-threshold wording: line 125 states eta_PT = -log(lambda_0) while line 211 states eta = -log(lambda_0) (missing 2*pi), and line 227 contains a nonsensical statement 'as soon as 2*pi*eta > 0' instead of the correct condition 2*pi*eta > L. Additionally, line 366-367 contains prose about turning points at eta = ±lambda_0 that is inconsistent with the correct turning points at eta = ±log(lambda_0)/(2*pi). These prose errors are quarantined and do not affect the selected Lyapunov equations, which are internally consistent. The reported value 0.119 for eta_PT at lambda_1=0.25, lambda_2=0.5 is retained as a reported value. Therefore case_authoring_ready is set to true.","case_authoring_ready":true,"claims":[{"anchors":[{"line_end":108,"line_start":108,"support":"This can be inferred from the Lyapunov exponent L_lambda1_lambda2_Phi of the model, which describes the average decay of eigenstates"},{"line_end":109,"line_start":109,"support":"In the localized phase, we have L = log(lambda_0) > 0"},{"line_end":111,"line_start":111,"support":"lambda_0 = lambda_2*(1+lambda_1')/(lambda_1*(1+lambda_2'))"},{"line_end":402,"line_start":402,"support":"L = max{0, log(lambda_0) - 2*pi*|eta|}"}],"applicability":"The claim applies to the Hermitian regime (eta=0) with theta=0 and Phi=(sqrt(5)-1)/2. The formula is phase-spanning: positive L indicates localization, zero L indicates delocalization.","claim_id":"lyapunov_eta_zero","control_domains":[{"allowed_values":[],"anchors":[{"line_end":82,"line_start":82,"support":"lambda_1=0.67, 0.435, 0.2 in the Hermitian quantum walk"},{"line_end":134,"line_start":134,"support":"we experimentally map the relationship between sigma and lambda_1, lambda_2 by scanning the whole parameter space"}],"control_name":"lambda_1","domain_kind":"continuous_interval","domain_statement":"The source reports lambda_1 values 0.67, 0.435, 0.2 in the Hermitian phase diagram; the conservative interval [0.2, 0.67] lies within the source's 0-to-1 coupling range and is covered by the reported full-parameter-space phase scans.","maximum":0.67,"minimum":0.2},{"allowed_values":[],"anchors":[{"line_end":82,"line_start":82,"support":"lambda_2=0.2, 0.435, 0.67 in the Hermitian quantum walk"},{"line_end":134,"line_start":134,"support":"we experimentally map the relationship between sigma and lambda_1, lambda_2 by scanning the whole parameter space"}],"control_name":"lambda_2","domain_kind":"continuous_interval","domain_statement":"The source reports lambda_2 values 0.2, 0.435, 0.67 in the Hermitian phase diagram; the conservative interval [0.2, 0.67] lies within the source's 0-to-1 coupling range and is covered by the reported full-parameter-space phase scans.","maximum":0.67,"minimum":0.2}],"control_names":["lambda_1","lambda_2"],"depends_on":[],"fixed_condition_ids":["irrational_field_phi","theta_zero","eta_zero"],"mechanism_ids":["lambda0_structure","lyapunov_eta_zero"],"oracle_outputs":{"lyapunov_exponent":{"args":[{"param":"zero"},{"args":[{"args":[{"args":[{"args":[{"var":"lambda_2"},{"args":[{"param":"one"},{"args":[{"args":[{"param":"one"},{"args":[{"var":"lambda_1"},{"param":"square_exponent"}],"op":"power"}],"op":"subtract"},{"param":"sqrt_exponent"}],"op":"power"}],"op":"add"}],"op":"multiply"},{"args":[{"var":"lambda_1"},{"args":[{"param":"one"},{"args":[{"args":[{"param":"one"},{"args":[{"var":"lambda_2"},{"param":"square_exponent"}],"op":"power"}],"op":"subtract"},{"param":"sqrt_exponent"}],"op":"power"}],"op":"add"}],"op":"multiply"}],"op":"divide"}],"op":"log"},{"args":[{"param":"two_pi"},{"param":"eta_zero"}],"op":"multiply"}],"op":"subtract"}],"op":"maximum"}},"quantitative_form":"L = max(0, log(lambda_0)) with lambda_0 = lambda_2*(1+sqrt(1-lambda_1^2))/(lambda_1*(1+sqrt(1-lambda_2^2)))","readout_names":["lyapunov_exponent"],"statement":"In the Hermitian regime (eta=0), the Lyapunov exponent of the UAMO is L = max(0, log(lambda_0)), where lambda_0 = lambda_2*(1+lambda_1')/(lambda_1*(1+lambda_2')). The positive versus zero output identifies localization versus delocalization.","statistical_basis":"The Lyapunov exponent is a theoretical quantity; the source reports the formula and uses it to characterize the localization transition. The experimental phase diagram is measured via the standard deviation sigma at t=6.","uncertainty":"source_unspecified"},{"anchors":[{"line_end":402,"line_start":402,"support":"L = max{0, log(lambda_0) - 2*pi*|eta|}"},{"line_end":425,"line_start":425,"support":"We set theta=0, eta=0.1"},{"line_end":111,"line_start":111,"support":"lambda_0 = lambda_2*(1+lambda_1')/(lambda_1*(1+lambda_2'))"}],"applicability":"The claim applies to the non-Hermitian regime with eta=0.1, theta=0 and Phi=(sqrt(5)-1)/2. The formula is phase-spanning: positive L indicates localization, zero L indicates delocalization.","claim_id":"lyapunov_eta_point_one","control_domains":[{"allowed_values":[],"anchors":[{"line_end":117,"line_start":117,"support":"lambda_1=0.5 and 0.25 in the non-Hermitian quantum walk"},{"line_end":418,"line_start